  - [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m also accepts [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer <token>[39m when it matches the [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m environment variable.
    The comparison is constant-time.
  - If [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m isn't set, the token route is off, so nothing opens up by accident.

  I started the server locally with a token set and called the route four ways:

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────┐
  │               Request               │       Result       │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────┤
  │ No credentials                      │ 401                │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────┤
  │ Correct token                       │ 200, data returned │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────┤
  │ Wrong token                         │ 401                │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mCookie: session=abc[39m (made-up value) │ 200, data returned │
  └─────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────┘

  To finish the setup, set [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m to a random value where the service is deployed (e.g. from [38;5;153mopenssl rand [39m
  [38;5;153m-hex 32[39m). Then give the dashboard team that value for their script, sent as [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer <token>[39m.

  The last row of the table is the cookie hole I mentioned earlier: any made-up [38;5;153msession=[39m cookie still gets in. Fixing it
  means verifying the signature in [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m, which needs to know how [38;5;153m/login[39m signs the cookie. [38;5;153m/login[39m isn't in this
  repo. I can make that change if you point me to it.

  I haven't committed anything.

